[Music]  this morning a special edition of our  morning rounds Medical News Alzheimer's  disease is a growing health crisis more  than five million Americans are  estimated to have it and that number  could top 15 million by 2050 British  neuroscientist Joseph chabela has  studied Alzheimer's throughout his  career and recently went on a journey of  discovery that produced his new book  that's out now in pursuit of memory the  fight against Alzheimer's dr. Joe jebel  II welcome hi to be with you what  motivated you to go on this this well it  turned out to be a very personal journey  didn't it did yeah so I first became  interested in out signers after seeing  it in my grandfather when I was a  teenager he was going through the  process of Alzheimer's disease and like  many people I just wanted answers you  know what is this disease what's  happening to him and how can we stop it  and that's so that's how I sort of  became interested in in the field and I  then sort of decided to write the book  because I wanted to give the public an  accessible account of where we are in  terms of the research you know what the  history of the research is and how we've  got to where we are and ultimately where  we're headed in the future by 2050 it is  expected to overtake cancer to be the  world's second leading cause of death  clearly something that's affecting  millions of people around the world what  is the difference between Alzheimer's  and dementia  sure so dementia just describes the set  of symptoms that you see in an out sinus  patient such as memory loss navigational  loss confusion just problems and general  thinking ability but out Simas describes  the underlying disease process so a good  way to think of it is is it's like it's  like saying oh so if you say someone has  dementia it's like saying they had  cancer without identifying which type of  cancer they had so out Symonds it's just  a type of dementia in the same way you  have different types of cancer you go  back to the origins and through the  history of Alzheimer's how much is the  attitude toward the disease changed over  the years Oh massively massively so so  you know as I document in the book it's  we've gone from essentially fear and  indifference to understanding and hope  when the German psychiatrist dr. alloy  out Simon described the disease in 1906  he was essentially ignored by his peers  because at the time the idea of linking  a biological manifestation in the brain  to a behavioral manifestation which is  the very unusual  alien concept it's very obvious to us  now so it took a long time for  researchers to finally wake up to this  problem and you know in the 60s and 70s  out science was essentially rediscovered  by some amazing psychiatrists like the  hungarian martin wroath and the english  microscopy Michael Kidd and since then  we've sort of had a Renault reawakening  about what outsiders is and  understanding that it is a disease  process and it's something that will  yield to science and reason in the same  way that cancer will and when you talk  about early warning signs you give  examples of somebody miss placing their  keys and being alarmed and you say  that's not what should alarm you what  should alarm you is if you don't  remember what keys are for yeah exactly  you know it's it's forgetting where your  car keys are your glasses are normal  everyone has every day for guessing as  they age and you probably weren't paying  enough attention to the memory you're  probably bit tired it's when you find  things like your keys and your glasses  and you think hang on what are these for  it's when that confusion sets in that's  when it's a sign of something a bit more  sinister and something that you should  then really consider you know going to  see a doctor about a loss of mobility  it's an early sign - yeah exactly loss  of navigation yeah it's a really great  research came out last year showing that  that's actually one of the first signs  even maybe before memory loss itself  that loss of navigation getting lost  might be one of the earliest signs you  talked about how we've moved from fear  towards hope how close are we to a  breakthrough at this point sure so the  most optimistic estimates and it's one  that I kind of ascribe to because I'm a  natural optimist is between 10 to 20  years that we will at least have an  effective treatment because what we  really need to do is push the illness  back you know the cure for Alzheimer's  is actually not what many people think  it'll be a treatment that just pushes it  back if we can push it back by a year  there'll be 9 million fewer cases of it  by 2030 well if we can push it back by 5  years that will essentially - half the  globe's 46 million sufferers of out  Simas disease now so really we just need  to push it back change the course of the  disease itself so the victim never has  to experience any of the symptoms of the  disease by pushing it back you mean they  they will succumb to something else  other than all time yes sort of died  naturally of old age you know they won't  have to go through this devastating  process in it you know in in their final  years what are we talking about a  vaccine a cure treatment will this be  chronic treatment the way we treat let's  say diabetes yes so it's it's seeming  more and more now that it's going to be  a treatment that there has to be given  very early we know that Alzheimer's is a  disease process that evolves over  decades of time and if it actually  starts maybe 10 even 20 years before you  see any symptoms and so now scientists  are looking for these very early  telltale signs and things like spinal  fluid the blood maybe even the eye and  trying to spot the disease in middle age  and then give a patient a drug at that  point right to divert the course of the  disease entirely quickly you went to  Iceland Colombia and India to research  this what did you learn there yeah I did  travel far and wide for the book I  essentially learnt that as a scientist  you know you really should leave no  stone unturned yeah you know who would  have thought that there's a population  of people in Iceland who are genetically  immune to our Symes disease who would  have thought that there are farming  communities in India that have to have  something in their lifestyle that  protects them from Alzheimer's who would  have thought that there are groups of  patients in Colombia with early onset  Alzheimer's that are helping shed light  on the cause of Alzheimer's disease so  it's you know it's we really do need to  widen the net in search of a cure  because we're you know this is a much  more complicated disease and we'd  previously thought and so I think as a  scientist that's what I took away from  it that we really should be searching  everywhere it's a fascinating book in  pursuit of memory that fight against  Alzheimer's Georgia Belly thanks for  being with us thank you

